        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">March First Movement</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a> to all thirteen provinces and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeju-island">Jeju Island</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-far-east">Russian Far East</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> among the Korean diaspora.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">Planned in late 1918 and early 1919; protests from 1 March 1919, concentrated in March and April; related protests continued until 1921.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Leading figures</span><span class="info-stat-val">The thirty-three signers of the Korean Declaration of Independence, among them the Cheondoist leaders Kwŏn Tongjin, O Se-chang and Son Byong-hi; the missionary Frank Schofield; the student Yu Gwan-sun.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> was not liberated promptly, but the Korean Provisional Government was founded, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s colonial government granted some limited cultural freedoms known as cultural rule, and the movement became central to Korean national memory.</span></div>
        </div>
