    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The movement began on 1 March 1919 with a public reading of the Korean Declaration of Independence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a> and spread within weeks to all thirteen provinces, in around 1,500 to 1,800 protests.</li><li>Organisers chose nonviolence and turned to international opinion, but the protests were frequently violently suppressed; one Korean estimate of 1920 claimed 7,509 deaths and 46,948 arrests, while Japanese authorities reported lower numbers.</li><li>It did not free <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, but it led to the Korean Provisional Government and to cultural rule, and it inspired other movements abroad, though how far is debated.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Canadian missionary Frank Schofield, who documented the protests and publicised them abroad, has since been described as the “Thirty-fourth Representative”, equating his significance to that of the thirty-three signers of the declaration.</p></aside>
    </section>

