    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1910</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> formally annexes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> and begins a process of forced cultural assimilation</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1918</span><span class="rf-text">After the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/woodrow-wilson">Woodrow Wilson</a> announces the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourteen-points">Fourteen Points</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/self-determination">national self-determination</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1919</span><span class="rf-text">Former emperor Gojong dies on 21 January, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koreans">Koreans</a> widely suspect that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> poisoned him</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1919</span><span class="rf-text">Students in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> proclaim a declaration of independence in February and are arrested en masse</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1919</span><span class="rf-text">Signers read the Korean Declaration of Independence at T’aehwagwan on 1 March, and a crowd at Tapgol Park begins a march along Jongno</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1919</span><span class="rf-text">In the Jeamni massacre on 15 April, Japanese authorities lure 20 to 30 civilians into a church, shoot them and burn it down</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1919</span><span class="rf-text">Korean exiles in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> found the Korean Provisional Government in April</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1949</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a> designates 1 March as the national holiday Samiljeol</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/woodrow-wilson"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/woodrow-wilson.jpg" alt="Woodrow Wilson" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/woodrow-wilson">Woodrow Wilson</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:President_Woodrow_Wilson_Harris_%26_Ewing_(3x4_cropped_b).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Harris &amp; Ewing, photographer, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 1910, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> formally annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>. Its rule was at first especially tight: Japan took control of Korea’s economy and began a process of Japanisation, forced cultural assimilation. Land was confiscated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koreans">Koreans</a> and given to Japanese people, and economic and administrative systems were created that were systemically discriminatory. Resistance was violently suppressed, and freedom of speech and press were tightly controlled. Koreans had to send growing portions of their output to Japan for what was seen as inadequate compensation, and the quality of life of many farmers and labourers declined. There were 6 strikes with 362 participants in 1916, 8 with 1,128 in 1917 and 50 with 4,442 in 1918, which historians of the National Institute of Korean History take as a sign of rising discontent.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> ended in 1918, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/woodrow-wilson">Woodrow Wilson</a> announced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourteen-points">Fourteen Points</a>, which included the right of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/self-determination">national self-determination</a>, and Koreans who learned of them interpreted them as support for their independence movement. Scholars debate Wilson’s sincerity: some argue that his advocacy was limited to the former colonies of the defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-powers">Central Powers</a>, while Ku Daeyeol argues that it was sincere but frustrated by geopolitical realities. The colonial government suppressed discussion of the Fourteen Points.</p><p>Koreans made several unsuccessful attempts to be represented at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-peace-conference-1919-1920">Paris Peace Conference</a>. On 21 January 1919 the former emperor Gojong died suddenly, and Koreans widely suspected that Japan had poisoned him, although Japan reported natural causes. Public outrage at the possibility of murder has been evaluated as critical to the timing of the movement.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aims">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aims</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/fourteen-points"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fourteen-points.jpg" alt="Fourteen Points" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/fourteen-points">Fourteen Points</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:William_Orpen_Woodrow_Wilson_cropped.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">William Orpen, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The movement sought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>’s independence from Japanese rule through nonviolent resistance and by turning international public opinion against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. In late 1918 the leaders of the native Korean religion Cheondoism, among them Kwŏn Tongjin, O Se-chang and Son Byong-hi, reached a consensus that this approach would be effective. They agreed that they needed help from other major groups in Korea, and sent representatives to secure the cooperation of politicians and organisations.</p><p>An earlier statement came from Korean students in Japan, who by the mid-1910s had been exposed to liberal democracy, partly through the Japanese Taishō Democracy movement and Wilson’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourteen-points">Fourteen Points</a>. In February 1919, 8,600 students of the Korean Young People’s Independence Organization proclaimed a declaration of independence and sent it to the Japanese government and to attendees of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-peace-conference-1919-1920">Paris Peace Conference</a>. The students were arrested en masse, though news of their act reached Korea.</p><p>The Korean Declaration of Independence, signed by thirty-three representatives from 25 to 27 February, contains the text “We entertain no spirit of vengeance towards Japan... [L]et there be no violence”. The organizers also sent copies to the Korean diaspora, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/woodrow-wilson">President Wilson</a> and to participants in the Paris Peace Conference. They had meant to start in Tapgol Park in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a>, but worried that angered onlookers might retaliate violently if the leaders were arrested there, so they moved the start, in the interest of nonviolence, to the less visible restaurant T’aehwagwan.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="events">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Course of Events</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhang-zuolin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zhang-zuolin.jpg" alt="Zhang Zuolin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/zhang-zuolin">Zhang Zuolin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zhangzuolin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">B. Nagakiya, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Around noon on 1 March 1919, the day of Gojong’s public funeral, 29 of the 33 signers gathered at T’aehwagwan and read the declaration. The restaurant owner An Sunhwan reported the event to the Japanese Government-General of Chōsen, and about 80 military police arrested the signers. Meanwhile 4,000 to 5,000 people had assembled at Tapgol Park, where around 2 p.m. an unidentified young man read the declaration aloud. The crowd cheered for Korean independence and marched onto the street Jongno, and by the time it reached the Daehanmun gate of Deoksugung palace it numbered tens of thousands.</p><p>That day protests were also held in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyongyang">Pyongyang</a>, Chinnamp’o, Anju and Wonsan, cities linked to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a> by railway. By 19 March all thirteen provinces had hosted protests, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeju-island">Jeju Island</a> held its first on 21 March, and all but seven of the 218 administrative districts took part. Protests often coincided with market days and were often held at government offices. Statistics are uncertain: around 1,500 to 1,800 protests with 0.8 to 2 million participants, in a population of around 16 to 17 million. A Japanese police report claimed 1,214 protests with 1.1 million participants.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koreans">Koreans</a> abroad joined. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> a large protest was held in Longjing on 13 March, with estimates of 20,000 to 30,000 people, and Japanese pressure on the warlord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-zuolin">Zhang Zuolin</a> led to around 17 to 19 deaths. Protests followed in Ussuriysk and in Sinhanch’on in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladivostok">Vladivostok</a>, and Koreans in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> held meetings.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="people">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">People</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/syngman-rhee"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/syngman-rhee.jpg" alt="Syngman Rhee" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/syngman-rhee">Syngman Rhee</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rhee_Syng-Man_in_1948.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Thirty-three representatives signed the declaration. Cheondoist leaders began the planning and secured alliances with major Christian and Buddhist groups and student organisations, and with some former officials of the Korean Empire, though the Joseon-era politicians Park Yung-hyo and Han Kyu-sŏl rebuffed them. Eleven of the 33 signers were from the Pyongan region. Presbyterian leaders in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyongyang">Pyongyang</a> had independently planned a protest, and their efforts were merged into the Cheondoist ones.</p><p>The protests were broadly supported by merchants, noblemen, literati, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kisaeng">kisaeng</a>, labourers, monks, Christians, Cheondoists, students and farmers. According to one estimate, 17% of those arrested were Christians, who made up less than 1% of the population, 58.4% were peasants and 3.9% were labourers. Women both led and joined many protests, and a group of female students wrote a public letter, “From Korean School Girls”, to world leaders. The 16-year-old Ewha Haktang student Yu Gwan-sun, who spread news of the protests through villages around Cheonan, was arrested and died of her injuries in Seodaemun Prison on 28 September 1920; she is remembered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a> as a martyr.</p><p>Foreigners took part too. Robert Grierson allowed secret meetings in his house in Sŏngjin, and Frank Schofield was asked to document the protests. The journalist Valentine S. McClatchy and the missionary Homer Hulbert publicised them in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, and Korean-American activists such as Henry Chung did the same. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syngman-rhee">Syngman Rhee</a> and Chung were refused permission to travel to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, and Kim Kyu-sik went with the Chinese delegation.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="outcomes">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Outcomes</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Government-General had not expected the protests and was surprised by their scale. It recruited firemen and railroad guards, and received more military police, police officers and army divisions from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, equipped with lethal weapons. There are numerous reports of authorities opening fire or conducting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayonet">bayonet</a> charges on unarmed protestors. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suwon">Suwon</a> and Anseong they reportedly burnt 276 homes, killed 29 and arrested around 2,000 people. On 15 April, in the Jeamni massacre, they lured 20 to 30 civilians into a church, opened fire and burned it down to hide the killings. There are reports of torture, executions and harsh prison conditions in Seodaemun Prison.</p><p>The numbers are disputed. One Korean estimate of 1920 claimed 7,509 deaths and 46,948 arrests. Japanese officials reported 553 people killed and more than 12,000 arrested from 1 March to 11 April. The historian Kwon Tae-eok argued that Japanese sources were incentivised to lower the numbers. The South Korean National Institute of Korean History’s database of 2019 gives deliberately conservative estimates.</p><p>The movement did not bring prompt liberation. Japan ran a disinformation campaign, denying the protests, portraying them as violent Bolshevik uprisings or blaming missionaries, and no major foreign government challenged Japanese rule. The colonial government did grant limited cultural freedoms, grouped as “cultural rule”, including permission for Korean newspapers such as The Dong-A Ilbo and The Chosun Ilbo. Commentators called these changes largely cosmetic; Michael Shin argues that colonial authority actually expanded, and by the late 1930s many concessions were retracted.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi.jpg" alt="Mahatma Gandhi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mahatma-Gandhi,_studio,_1931.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Elliott &amp; Fry, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The legacy of the movement is still actively debated by scholars. It invigorated the Korean independence movement, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koreans">Koreans</a> who fled repression, many of them to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a>, founded the Korean Provisional Government in April 1919, now considered a predecessor of the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a>. Michael Shin views the movement as a turning point for Korean nationalism, from elite leadership to popular action. The 1926 June Tenth Movement had many similar protest actions.</p><p>The movement is widely seen in South Korea as a landmark in the formation of modern Korean national identity, and the constitution’s first sentence mentions the “March First Independence Movement of 1919”. South Korea designated 1 March a national holiday in 1949. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-korea">North Korea</a> made it a holiday in 1946, later demoted it, and teaches that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kim-il-sung">Kim Il Sung</a>’s family took the lead, an account that downplays the figures who later became influential in South Korea. Scholars in both North and South largely agree that the movement was unlikely to lead to prompt liberation.</p><p>Its effect on other movements is debated. Fu Ssu-nien and other organizers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>’s May Fourth Movement cited it as an inspiration, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a> is reported to have read of the protests and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> to launch non-cooperation. Some historians note that these protests arose from different causes. Youngseo Baik argues that the movement can be considered a revolution. In August 2015 the former Japanese prime minister Yukio Hatoyama apologised at Seodaemun Prison for the treatment of its prisoners.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The March First Movement was a wave of mostly peaceful protests against Japanese rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, begun on 1 March 1919 with a reading of the Korean Declaration of Independence. It was violently suppressed and did not free Korea, but it led to the Korean Provisional Government and to cultural rule.</em></p>

